{"records":[{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ynd8jkd367","title":"Data for: 3605888","description":"This study evaluates possible impacts of SPRR/A residues in viruses including SARS-CoV-2. An analysis on furin-cleavage proteins transcripts has been discussed in the light of available transcription data set from SARS-CoV-2 infected human cells. Based on sequence homology of virus interacting domain of ACE2 receptor, a sensitive host range has been predicted. Possible impacts of mutation in S gene has been analyzed keeping in view that antisense RNA mediated inhibition of cellular transcripts might be operated in the host cell. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-20T00:36:28.835Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ynd8jkd367","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ynd8jkd367"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"M. Irfan","last_name":"Qureshi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Biophysics","Bioinformatics","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":["15565068"]},{"id":"jx2x925mhw","title":"SARS-CoV-2 infects human pancreatic β-cells and elicits β-cell impairment. Wu et al.","description":"Emerging evidence points towards an intricate relationship between the pandemic coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and diabetes. While pre-existing diabetes is associated with severe COVID-19, recent evidence suggests COVID-19 induces new-onset type 1 diabetes (T1D), convoluting diabetes as cause or consequence. To mechanistically link COVID-19 to T1D, we tested whether insulin-producing pancreatic β-cells can be infected by SARS-CoV-2 and cause β-cell depletion. We find the SARS-CoV-2 receptor, ACE2, and related entry factors (TMPRSS2, NRP1, TRFC) are expressed in β-cells, with NRP1 selectively high. We discover that SARS-CoV-2 infects human pancreatic β-cells in patients who succumbed to COVID-19 and selectively infects human islet β-cells in vitro. We demonstrate SARS-CoV-2 infection attenuates pancreatic insulin levels and secretion and induces β-cell apoptosis, each rescued by NRP1 inhibition. Phosphoproteomic pathway analysis of infected islets indicates apoptotic β-cell signaling, similar to T1D. Our study shows SARS-CoV-2 can directly induce β-cell killing, explaining T1D in COVID-19 patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-04T11:19:12.889Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jx2x925mhw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jx2x925mhw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chien-Ting","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r52v2drd9b","title":"Aguilar-Pineda et al. Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry","description":"This dataset contains all the raw data and codes used for th adquision of the data presented in the manuscript: \"Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry \" by Aguilar-Pineda et al.\nHere we report new insights into the molecular basis of the interactions between the SARS-CoV-2 spike (S) protein and the human ACE2 receptor. We further report that glycosylation of the ACE2 receptor enhances SARS-CoV-2 infectivity. Importantly estrogens can disrupt glycan-glycan interactions and glycan-protein interactions between the human ACE2 and the SARS-CoV2 thereby blocking its entry into cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-11T13:04:31.536Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r52v2drd9b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r52v2drd9b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jorge Alberto","last_name":"Aguilar Pineda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin J.","last_name":"Vera López","orcid":"0000-0002-7908-8230"},{"first_name":"Rita M.","last_name":"Nieto-Montesinos","orcid":"0000-0002-5671-4593"},{"first_name":"Gonzalo H. ","last_name":"Davila-Del Carpio","orcid":"0000-0001-7842-9286"},{"first_name":"Badhin","last_name":"Gómez Valdez","orcid":"0000-0001-6539-1207"},{"first_name":"Christian L. ","last_name":"Lino Cardenas","orcid":"0000-0001-5491-4375"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Structural Biology","Estrogen","Molecular Dynamics Study","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tr2zxk67yg","title":"Binding and molecular basis of bat-RaTG13 virus to 25-species ACE2s including hACE2 , a study of K. Liu et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) has been spreading worldwide and causing a global pandemic. Bat-origin RaTG13 is currently the most phylogenetically related virus. Here, we obtained the complex structure of RaTG13 receptor binding domain (RBD) with human ACE2 (hACE2), and further evaluated the binding of RaTG13 RBD to 24 additional ACE2 orthologs. By substituting residues in RaTG13 RBD with their counterparts in SARS-CoV-2 RBD, we found that residue 501, the major position found in VOCs 501Y.V1/V2/V3, plays a key role in determining the potential host range of RaTG13. We also found that SARS-CoV-2 could induce strong cross-reactive antibodies to RaTG13 and identified a SARS-CoV-2 MAb, CB6, that could cross-neutralize RaTG13 pseudovirus. These results elucidate the receptor binding and host-adaption mechanisms of RaTG13 and emphasize the importance of continuous surveillance of coronaviruses (CoVs) carried by animal reservoirs to prevent another spill-over of CoVs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-30T14:08:11.004Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tr2zxk67yg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tr2zxk67yg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kefang","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoqian ","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Linjie","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Feng","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anqi","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pei","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pengcheng ","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumin","last_name":"Meng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanfang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lili","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chunli","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yunfei","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sheng","last_name":"Niu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dan","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chengpeng","last_name":"Qiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhihai","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dongli","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaopeng","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuguang","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianxun","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"George F. ","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qihui","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v6shwssh3s","title":"An ACAT inhibitor suppresses SARS-CoV-2 replication and boosts antiviral T cell activity","description":"The severity of disease following infection with SARS-CoV-2 is determined by viral replication kinetics and host immunity, with early T cell responses and/or suppression of viraemia driving a favourable outcome. Recent studies uncovered a role for cholesterol metabolism in the SARS-CoV-2 life cycle and in T cell function. Here we show that blockade of the enzyme Acyl-CoA:cholesterol acyltransferase (ACAT) with Avasimibe inhibits SARS-CoV-2 pseudoparticle infection and disrupts the association of ACE2 and GM1 lipid rafts on the cell membrane, perturbing viral attachment. Imaging SARS-CoV-2 RNAs at the single cell level using a viral replicon model identifies the capacity of Avasimibe to limit the establishment of replication complexes required for RNA replication. Genetic studies to transiently silence or overexpress ACAT isoforms confirmed a role for ACAT in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Furthermore, Avasimibe boosts the expansion of functional SARS-CoV-2-specific T cells from the blood of patients sampled during the acute phase of infection. Thus, re-purposing of ACAT inhibitors provides a compelling therapeutic strategy for the treatment of COVID-19 to achieve both antiviral and immunomodulatory effects.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-21T07:51:27.907Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v6shwssh3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v6shwssh3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peter ","last_name":"Wing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathalie","last_name":"Schmidt","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Virology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cysvc62njp","title":"Oral Ursodeoxycholic Acid Therapy & BA.5.2 Infection","description":"Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) was reported to reduce susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 infection by downregulating farnesoid X receptor (FXR) -ACE2 signaling. However, we found a different story in real-world clinical studies. We attempted to verify whether UDCA can effectively prevent SARS-CoV-2 transmission or have positive therapeutic effects in a real-world clinical study. We performed a retrospective study, collected, and assessed clinical presentation and laboratory data on patients with liver diseases infected with SARS-CoV-2 Omicron sub-variant BA.5.2 who had been treated with or without UDCA. Treatment with UDCA did not prevent infection with the Omicron sub-variant BA.5.2, failed in reducing the duration of infection and hardly mitigated the severity of COVID-19. Meanwhile, the severity of liver diseases, especially TBil, ALP, γ-GT, liver cirrhosis and Child-Pugh classification, should be considered as risk factors for severe COVID-19 in chronic hepatic patients. In conclusion, UDCA failed to show inhibitory effects against SARS-CoV-2 infection in complex clinical settings. The regulatory mechanism of the novel UDCA-FXR-ACE2 pathway needs to be further investigated in real-world clinical studies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-30T18:14:28.916Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cysvc62njp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cysvc62njp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dong-shan","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n9cph7hvrf","title":"A study of Systematic analysis of SARS-CoV-2 infection of an ACE2 negative human airway cell. Puray-Chavez et al.","description":"Supplemental tables for Puray-Chavez et al. Systematic analysis of SARS-CoV-2 infection of an ACE2 negative human airway cell. Published in Cell Reports.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-17T13:37:39.814Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n9cph7hvrf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n9cph7hvrf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kyle","last_name":"LaPak","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141131,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":190,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141131,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":140383,"restricted":69,"embargoed":679},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2349},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1453},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":698},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":482},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":446},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":431},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":395},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":283}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138472},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1910},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":139},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":945},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":918},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":531},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":365}]}}